            <div xml:id="d431">

                <pb facs="0691" n="577" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg577"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">662.6331/78: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Belgium</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gibson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Brussels</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-03-31T14:00:00+00:00">March 31, 1931—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 31—1:05 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>22. Department’s 15, March 30, 1 p.m. I am advised by the Foreign Office that,
                    when the Belgo-Luxemburg customs union was established in 1921, Belgium had
                    commercial treaties containing unconditional most-favored-nation clauses with
                    France, Great Britain, and the Netherlands. If there were others, the Foreign
                    Office will give me that information at a later date.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gibson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
